{-
   What the atomic operations *compute* - Analysis-Atomics.md §3.4 and §5.3.

   `resolve/Atomic.yana` pins the IR each of these lowers to and `x64/Atomic.lower` pins the bytes.
   Neither of those runs anything, and on this backend most of §5.3's table is not the instruction
   the IR names: a sequential store is an exchange, a subtraction whose result is read is the
   addition of a negation, and a bitwise update whose result is read is a compare-exchange **loop**
   built out of blocks the source never wrote. A loop that spins forever and one that answers the
   value from after the update instead of before it both pass a golden that was generated from them.

   So this file runs them. Every assertion below is a number, and the numbers are the ones the
   operation is defined to answer rather than the ones it happened to.

   One thread, which is not a limitation of what is being checked here. What an atomic operation
   promises under contention is indivisibility and ordering, and neither is something a single
   thread can observe - so what is checkable is the *value* half of the definition, which is the half
   the lowering can get wrong. The loop above runs one iteration here and the same instructions on
   every one of them.

   Native only, like the module: there is no `Atomic.yana.js` because there is no `Atomic.js.yana`.
-}

import Atomic
import Native

-- `store` and `exchange` are declared by `Native` too, so both are written qualified - see
-- `resolve/Atomic.yana`, where the same collision is explained.

-- A structure holding one, for `ownership` at the bottom. A second field beside it deliberately:
-- what makes the field case worth checking is that the atomic is *part* of something, so a record
-- with nothing else in it would not be the shape a program actually builds.
data Holder = Holder {flag: Atomic(Int), tag: Int}

{-
   Construction, consumption and the three basic operations.

   `atomic` and `intoValue` are the same bits read under the other type and emit no atomic
   instruction at all, so what this checks of them is that the value survives the round trip - in
   both directions, and past every operation in between.

   The sequential store is the one that is not the instruction it says: `expandAtomics` rewrites it
   into an exchange, whose result nothing reads. A rewrite that lost the value would show up here
   and nowhere else in the suite.
-}
fn basics() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(0)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0)

    Atomic.store(c, 7, StoreRelease)
    assert(c.load(LoadAcquire) == 7)

    Atomic.store(c, 11, StoreSequential)
    assert(c.load(LoadSequential) == 11)

    assert(Atomic.exchange(c, 9, UpdateAcquireRelease) == 11)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 9)
    assert(intoValue(c) == 9)

{-
   The two arithmetic updates, which answer the value from **before** the update.

   Both halves of each are here. Where the previous value is read, `fetchAdd` is `lock xadd` and
   `fetchSub` is a negation in front of one; where it is not, both are the locked in-place form and
   the only evidence they ran at all is what the location holds afterwards. So each is called both
   ways and the location is read between them.

   The subtraction is the one worth being exact about: negating the amount and adding is the same
   operation only if the negation is of the *amount* and not of the result, and the two differ in
   everything this asserts.
-}
fn arithmetic() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(1)

    assert(c.fetchAdd(4, UpdateRelaxed) == 1)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 5)

    assert(c.fetchSub(2, UpdateAcquire) == 5)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 3)

    -- And the same two with nobody reading the answer.
    c.fetchAdd(10, UpdateRelease)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 13)

    c.fetchSub(3, UpdateSequential)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 10)

-- Wrapping at the stored width, which is what §3.4 says the two of them do. A `U8` at 250 plus 10 is
-- 4, and the answer is still the value from before.
fn wrapping() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(250 :: U8)

    assert(c.fetchAdd(10, UpdateRelaxed) == 250)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 4)

    assert(c.fetchSub(10, UpdateRelaxed) == 4)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 250)

{-
   The three bitwise updates, which is the row of §5.3 that has no instruction.

   x86 has a locked `and`, `or` and `xor` that update a location and answer nothing, and nothing at
   all that both updates and hands back what was there. So a `fetchAnd` whose result is read becomes
   a compare-exchange loop: read, compute, try, and go round again when somebody else won. What the
   loop has to get right is that it answers the value it *read* rather than the one it stored, and
   that it leaves after one attempt when nothing is competing with it.

   Read and discarded are separate calls again, since they are separate instructions.
-}
fn bitwise() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(0b1111 :: U32)

    assert(c.fetchAnd(0b1010, UpdateRelease) == 0b1111)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b1010)

    assert(c.fetchOr(0b0101, UpdateAcquireRelease) == 0b1010)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b1111)

    assert(c.fetchXor(0b0110, UpdateSequential) == 0b1111)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b1001)

    c.fetchAnd(0b1000, UpdateRelaxed)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b1000)

    c.fetchOr(0b0011, UpdateRelaxed)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b1011)

    c.fetchXor(0b1111, UpdateRelaxed)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b0100)

{-
   The same three at every width the family admits.

   The loop's comparison is what the narrow widths are here for. `lock cmpxchg` with a one-byte
   operand writes AL and leaves the rest of the register alone, and the comparison standing in for
   its success flag is 32 bits wide - so the loop terminates only because the bits above the byte
   hold the expected value on both sides. That is an argument about the allocator's copies, and this
   is what turns it into a test: a loop that got it wrong would not answer a wrong number, it would
   not answer at all.
-}
fn widths() -> {}:
    let byte = atomic(0b1111 :: U8)
    assert(byte.fetchAnd(0b0110, UpdateRelaxed) == 0b1111)
    assert(byte.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0b0110)

    let half = atomic(0xF0F0 :: U16)
    assert(half.fetchXor(0xFFFF, UpdateAcquire) == 0xF0F0)
    assert(half.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0x0F0F)

    let word = atomic(0x0000FFFF :: U32)
    assert(word.fetchOr(0xFFFF0000, UpdateRelease) == 0x0000FFFF)
    assert(word.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0xFFFFFFFF)

    let long = atomic(0x00000000FFFFFFFF :: I64)
    assert(long.fetchAnd(0x00000000FF00FF00, UpdateSequential) == 0x00000000FFFFFFFF)
    assert(long.load(LoadRelaxed) == 0x00000000FF00FF00)

{-
   The signed narrow types, which are the case none of the above reaches.

   An unsigned value narrower than a register is held masked and a signed one **sign-extended**, and
   an atomic update answers neither for free: the register comes back holding the location's low
   bytes over whatever the instruction left above them - the addend's own high bits after a
   `lock xadd`, the expected value's after a `lock cmpxchg`. For an unsigned type those happen to be
   zero often enough that a wrong lowering passes; for a signed one they are ones as soon as anything
   is negative, and every assertion below is an equality against a negative number for that reason.

   `-5 + -1` is `-6`, and the previous value is `-5`. A register that came back as 0xFFFFFFFB where
   the value is 0xFB compares equal to neither.
-}
fn signedNarrow() -> {}:
    let zero = 0 :: I8
    let c = atomic(zero - 5)

    assert(c.fetchAdd(zero - 1, UpdateRelease) == zero - 5)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == zero - 6)

    assert(c.fetchSub(zero - 2, UpdateAcquire) == zero - 6)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == zero - 4)

    -- The bitwise loop at a signed byte, whose comparison is what closes it.
    assert(c.fetchAnd(zero - 1, UpdateSequential) == zero - 4)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == zero - 4)

    assert(c.fetchXor(zero - 1, UpdateRelaxed) == zero - 4)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 3)

    -- And the compare-exchange, whose failing arm hands back the value that was there - which is
    -- where the expected value's sign extension is standing in the register the answer arrives in.
    let d = atomic(zero - 100)

    match d.compareExchange(zero - 99, 7, UpdateAcquireRelease):
        Exchanged -> assert(False)
        Unchanged(->actual) -> assert(actual == zero - 100)

    match d.compareExchange(zero - 100, zero - 101, UpdateAcquireRelease):
        Exchanged -> {}
        Unchanged(->_) -> assert(False)

    assert(d.load(LoadAcquire) == zero - 101)

    -- The signed half-word, whose normalization is the same pair at a different distance.
    let wide = 0 :: I16
    let h = atomic(wide - 1000)
    assert(h.fetchAdd(wide - 1, UpdateRelaxed) == wide - 1000)
    assert(h.load(LoadRelaxed) == wide - 1001)

{-
   Compare-exchange, both outcomes.

   The failing one is the half a single call cannot check: it has to answer `Unchanged` carrying
   what was actually there, and leave the location alone. Losing the observed value is what would
   stop a retry loop from ever making progress, and answering `Exchanged` when nothing was stored is
   what would make one corrupt.
-}
fn compare() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(5)

    -- The comparison fails: nothing is stored and the value that was there comes back.
    match c.compareExchange(4, 99, UpdateAcquireRelease):
        Exchanged -> assert(False)
        Unchanged(->actual) -> assert(actual == 5)

    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 5)

    -- And succeeds.
    match c.compareExchange(5, 6, UpdateAcquireRelease):
        Exchanged -> {}
        Unchanged(->_) -> assert(False)

    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 6)

    -- The weak form, which on this target is the same instruction - so it succeeds here too, and a
    -- spurious failure would be a target that has one rather than a defect.
    match c.compareExchangeWeak(6, 7, UpdateRelease):
        Exchanged -> {}
        Unchanged(->_) -> {}

    -- `Advanced`'s two-order spelling, whose failure order is stated rather than derived.
    match Advanced.compareExchange(c, 7, 8, UpdateSequential, LoadAcquire):
        Exchanged -> assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 8)
        Unchanged(->actual) -> assert(actual == 7)

{-
   A retry loop written the way an algorithm writes one, which is what the compare-exchange surface
   exists for: the failed attempt's observed value becomes the next attempt's expectation.

   Uncontended, so it wins first time - and the loop is here for the shape rather than for the
   contention. What it checks is that the answer feeds back in without a second read of the location,
   which is the property §3.4 fixes and the reason `Unchanged` carries a value at all.
-}
fn retry() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(10)
    let &expected = c.load(LoadRelaxed)
    let &turns = 0

    while True:
        turns = turns + 1
        match c.compareExchangeWeak(expected, expected * 2, UpdateAcquireRelease):
            Exchanged -> break
            Unchanged(->actual) -> expected = actual

    assert(c.load(LoadAcquire) == 20)
    assert(turns == 1)

{-
   A payload-free enumeration in an atomic - the case a mutex's state word is, and the reason the
   family admits any of them rather than `Bool` alone.

   The storage is the enumeration's own tag rounded up to a power of two, so `State` is a byte and
   `Code` is two. What that has to preserve is constructor identity through an exchange and through a
   comparison, which is what a bitwise compare over a tag word is exactly.
-}
data State = Idle | Locked | Contended
data Code = @value(0) Ok | @value(1000) Retry | @value(60000) Fatal

fn enums() -> {}:
    let state = atomic(Idle)

    match state.compareExchange(Idle, Locked, UpdateAcquire):
        Exchanged -> {}
        Unchanged(->_) -> assert(False)

    assert(state.load(LoadAcquire) == Locked)

    -- The contended path, where the expectation is wrong and the constructor that was there comes
    -- back rather than the one that was asked for.
    match state.compareExchange(Idle, Contended, UpdateAcquire):
        Exchanged -> assert(False)
        Unchanged(->actual) -> assert(actual == Locked)

    assert(Atomic.exchange(state, Idle, UpdateSequential) == Locked)
    assert(intoValue(state) == Idle)

    -- And the two-byte one, whose pinned numbers are far enough apart that a byte would lose them.
    let code = atomic(Ok)
    Atomic.store(code, Retry, StoreRelease)
    assert(code.load(LoadAcquire) == Retry)
    assert(Atomic.exchange(code, Fatal, UpdateAcquireRelease) == Retry)
    assert(code.load(LoadRelaxed) == Fatal)

-- A flag, which is the enumeration `Bool` already is - and a pointer, which is the family's other
-- member and the one a lock-free structure actually swings.
fn flagsAndPointers(p: %U8) -> {}:
    let flag = atomic(False)
    assert(Atomic.exchange(flag, True, UpdateAcquireRelease) == False)
    assert(flag.load(LoadAcquire) == True)

    let empty = null() :: %U8
    let address = atomic(empty)
    Atomic.store(address, p, StoreRelease)
    assert(Atomic.exchange(address, empty, UpdateAcquireRelease) == p)
    assert(intoValue(address) == empty)

{-
   A fence and a spin hint, which compute nothing.

   Neither is asserted about, because there is nothing about either that one thread can observe -
   §3.6 says as much of the spin hint in so many words. What this checks is the one thing that can
   go wrong with an instruction whose whole content is its effect on other instructions: that it
   emits something the processor accepts, and that the accesses around it still happen.
-}
fn ordering() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(0)

    Atomic.store(c, 1, StoreRelaxed)
    fence(FenceRelease)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 1)

    fence(FenceAcquire)
    fence(FenceAcquireRelease)
    fence(FenceSequential)
    spinHint()

    assert(intoValue(c) == 1)

{-
   `Native.atomicAt` - §3.7, the unchecked entrance.

   A view of bytes this file owns rather than of a mapping, because what is being checked is the
   *view*: that the operations reach the location the pointer named, that what an ordinary read of
   those bytes sees afterwards is what the atomic wrote, and that the two directions agree. A memory
   mapping would test the operating system.

   The four promises §3.7 lists are kept here by construction. The storage outlives every borrow
   taken of it, `U32` is naturally aligned inside a `U32` slot, the bytes are initialized before the
   first view, and nothing else touches them while a view is live.

   `intoValue` is deliberately absent: it consumes an atomic, and a view owns nothing to consume.
-}
fn viewed() -> {}:
    let &storage = 0 :: U32
    let p = bitcast(addressOf(storage)) :: %U8
    let view = Native.atomicAt(p) :: 'Atomic(U32)

    Atomic.store(view, 7, StoreRelease)
    assert(view.load(LoadAcquire) == 7)

    -- What the location holds, read the ordinary way. The view is the same bytes and not a copy of
    -- them, which is the whole of what the operation claims.
    assert(storage == 7)

    assert(view.fetchAdd(5, UpdateRelaxed) == 7)
    assert(storage == 12)

    -- And the other direction: a plain write is what the next atomic read sees.
    storage = 100
    assert(view.load(LoadAcquire) == 100)

    match view.compareExchange(100, 101, UpdateAcquireRelease):
        Exchanged -> {}
        Unchanged(->_) -> assert(False)

    assert(storage == 101)

-- Two views of one location, which is the case the whole surface exists for: an atomic is the one
-- thing many holders may mutate at once, so `atomicAt` answers a *shared* borrow and two of them
-- coexist. Their updates accumulate on the location rather than on either of them.
fn shared() -> {}:
    let &storage = 0 :: U32
    let p = bitcast(addressOf(storage)) :: %U8

    let first = Native.atomicAt(p) :: 'Atomic(U32)
    let second = Native.atomicAt(p) :: 'Atomic(U32)

    first.fetchAdd(3, UpdateRelaxed)
    second.fetchAdd(4, UpdateRelaxed)

    assert(first.load(LoadRelaxed) == 7)
    assert(second.load(LoadRelaxed) == 7)
    assert(storage == 7)

{-
   That a borrow and a field are the **same location**, and not a copy of one.

   `resolve/AtomicOwnership.yana` pins which of these the compiler admits and
   `resolve/Reject.AtomicOwnership.yana` which it refuses, and neither can see what is being checked
   here: an atomic that were quietly duplicated on its way into a shared loan or a record field would
   pass both of those files and every ordering assertion above it. What it would fail is this - the
   writes would land in one location and the reads in another.

   Which is not a hypothetical shape. Recording a hand-over only for a type that needs teardown left
   `Holder {flag: c}` copying the atomic into the field, and what that produces is exactly two
   counters that a reader of either would call the counter.
-}
fn viaLoan(c: 'Atomic(Int)) -> {}:
    c.fetchAdd(5, UpdateRelaxed)

fn ownership() -> {}:
    let c = atomic(1)

    -- Through a shared loan, twice, with the original read in between and afterwards. One location
    -- or three is the whole question, and only the first answers 11.
    viaLoan(c)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 6)

    viaLoan(c)
    assert(c.load(LoadRelaxed) == 11)

    -- And through a field, after the atomic has been moved into one. The record owns it now, so
    -- what the loan reaches has to be the storage the constructor took over rather than a copy of
    -- the bytes it was built from.
    let h = Holder {flag: c, tag: 2}
    viaLoan(h.flag)

    -- And not `intoValue(h.flag)`, which is a partial move and refused - the whole record is what
    -- moves. `resolve/Reject.AtomicOwnership.yana` is where that is pinned.
    assert(h.flag.load(LoadRelaxed) == 16)

fn main() -> Int:
    basics()
    arithmetic()
    wrapping()
    bitwise()
    widths()
    signedNarrow()
    compare()
    retry()
    enums()
    flagsAndPointers(null())
    ordering()
    viewed()
    shared()
    ownership()
    return 0
